    <!-- 6 PROPERTY TREND (8.5) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Property trends · 8.5</span>
      <h2>Trends down the column</h2>
      <p>As the hydrocarbon <b>chains get longer</b> (more carbon and hydrogen atoms), the forces between molecules get stronger. So down the column:</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="As chain length increases, boiling point and viscosity rise but ease of ignition falls">
          <line x1="40" y1="150" x2="520" y2="150" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <polygon points="520,150 506,144 506,156" fill="#2c2840"/>
          <text x="280" y="172" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">longer chain  →  more carbon &amp; hydrogen atoms</text>
          <g font-family="Fredoka" font-size="13" font-weight="600">
            <text x="60" y="40" fill="#c8203a">▲ boiling point — higher</text>
            <text x="60" y="68" fill="#9a5a00">▲ viscosity — thicker, flows less easily</text>
            <text x="60" y="96" fill="#0b5bbf">▼ ease of ignition — harder to light, burns less cleanly</text>
            <text x="60" y="124" fill="#6a30b8">▼ volatility — evaporates less easily</text>
          </g>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Short-chain fractions (gases, petrol) ignite easily and are runny; long-chain fractions (fuel oil, bitumen) are thick and hard to ignite.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="note"><b>Why?</b> Longer molecules have larger surfaces, so the <b>intermolecular forces</b> are stronger. More energy is needed to separate them — raising the boiling point and viscosity, and making them harder to ignite.</p>
    </section>

