    <!-- 14 HYDROGEN FUEL (8.14) -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Hydrogen as a fuel · 8.14</span>
      <h2>Hydrogen vs petrol</h2>
      <p>Hydrogen can be used instead of petrol in cars. You should be able to <b>evaluate</b> the trade-offs:</p>
      <div class="match" style="margin-bottom:14px">
        <div class="mcol">
          <div class="mhead" style="color:#137a45">✔ Advantages</div>
          <div class="mitem" style="pointer-events:none;background:#eafaf0">Burning it makes only <b>water</b> — no CO₂, CO, soot or SO₂</div>
          <div class="mitem" style="pointer-events:none;background:#eafaf0">Can be made from water; not a finite oil fraction</div>
        </div>
        <div class="mcol">
          <div class="mhead" style="color:#c8203a">✘ Disadvantages</div>
          <div class="mitem" style="pointer-events:none;background:#fdeef0">Hard to <b>store</b> &amp; transport (high-pressure gas, very flammable)</div>
          <div class="mitem" style="pointer-events:none;background:#fdeef0">Few filling stations; energy is needed to <b>make</b> the hydrogen</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="note"><b>Balanced view:</b> hydrogen is much cleaner <b>at the point of use</b>, but whether it is "green" overall depends on <b>how the hydrogen was produced</b> — and on the storage and refuelling network catching up.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 15 EARLY ATMOSPHERE (8.18-8.22) -->
    <section class="screen" data-teach="atmos">
      <span class="tag lo">Early atmosphere · 8.18–8.22</span>
      <h2>How the atmosphere evolved</h2>
      <p>The Earth's <b>early atmosphere</b> is thought to have come from intense <b>volcanic activity</b>, which released large amounts of <b>carbon dioxide</b> and <b>water vapour</b>, with <b>little or no oxygen</b>.</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 180" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Over billions of years carbon dioxide fell and oxygen rose">
          <!-- early -->
          <circle cx="100" cy="90" r="46" fill="#e8d6c0" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="100" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="#9a5a00">🌋 early Earth</text>
          <text x="100" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#c8203a">CO₂ high</text>
          <text x="100" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="10.5" fill="#6b6580">O₂ ≈ none</text>
          <text x="100" y="160" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#6b6580">volcanic gases</text>
          <!-- arrows / processes -->
          <line x1="160" y1="90" x2="400" y2="90" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/><polygon points="400,90 386,83 386,97" fill="#2c2840"/>
          <text x="280" y="44" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#137a45">oceans formed → CO₂ dissolved in</text>
          <text x="280" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#137a45">the water (CO₂ falls)</text>
          <text x="280" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#0b5bbf">algae &amp; plants photosynthesise →</text>
          <text x="280" y="134" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="11" fill="#0b5bbf">use CO₂, release O₂ (O₂ rises)</text>
          <!-- now -->
          <circle cx="470" cy="90" r="46" fill="#cfe7ff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="470" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12.5" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">🌍 today</text>
          <text x="470" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#0b5bbf">O₂ ~21%</text>
          <text x="470" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="10.5" fill="#6b6580">CO₂ ~0.04%</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>CO₂ <b>fell</b> (dissolved into the new oceans) while O₂ <b>rose</b> as algae and plants photosynthesised.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <!-- 16 TODAY'S ATMOSPHERE composition -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Today's atmosphere</span>
      <h2>What the air is made of now</h2>
      <p>Billions of years of change have left the modern atmosphere remarkably steady. Roughly:</p>
      <figure>
